Date: Sun, 11 Apr 93 23:48:38 EDT
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: Economic systems

   From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu

   Could you give an example of modern Communist governments (1900-1970)
   which were "reasonably successful"? I can't seem to recall any, unless
   one believes the revisionist rhetoric about the soviet union in the
   1930s. Please back up such examples with good references.
   (soviet union data isn't very good because they had very poor
   accounting)

Well, I'm using a fairly loose definition of "reasonably successful".
The Soviet Union *survived* for sixty years or so, managed to fight a
brutal and exhausting war, and made a pretty good go at having a
world-class military machine for thirty or fourty years after that.
Stalin did industrialize the country, although I'm sure the figures
were inflated.  As recently as a few years ago, the Soviet Union was
sending significant amounts of economic aid to Cuba, Vietnam, and
perhaps other countries.

What I find interesting is the transition between "reasonably
successful" and "absolutely falling apart".  Yes, a lot of it was due
to accumulated internal rot, and perhaps due to not having a recent
ruler as ruthlessly efficient as Stalin.  But the world economy in the
last 20 years or so has been changing faster and faster, which it a
situation which maximizes the competitive weakness of a command
economy vs. a market economy.  I suspect that if the world economy was
still idling at the rates of change prevelant in the 1960s, the Soviet
Union might well still be intact, and drag on for another 50 years.

In a broader sense, the accellerating world economy is likely to get
better and better at competing in more and more niches, and will
probably eliminate more and more pockets of command and traditional
economies.

Dale

