Date: Mon, 12 Apr 1993 00:13:47 -0400 (EDT)
From: cbmvax!snark.thyrsus.com!esr@uunet.UU.NET (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: thinking isn't what you think it is

>                                                                    Might
> there be analogous logical illusions?  I think it not unlikely that some
> mathematical proofs rest on flaws that we simply cannot see -- after all,
> the only assurance we have of a proof's correctness is that mathematicians
> have looked at it and not seen flaws.

I was a meta-mathematician once.  We worry about things like this.

It is obvious that there are "logical illusions" we are all wired for ---
false presuppositions about logical deduction that one must consciously
unlearn, and which tend to reassert themselves unless one maintains a fairly
careful mental discipline.

Perhaps the best known is the tendency to confuse independent-trial
probability with non-replacement probability --- that is, the coin-flip case
vs. the marbles-in-a-jar case.  I could multiply examples, but this one
suffices to demonstate existence.  And it's not a trivial or marginal error,
either --- it plays hell not just with gamblers but with any kind of rational
risk assessment.

However, the existence of such "logical illusions" is not much of a threat
to mathematical proof, because the integrity of mathematical proof doesn't
depend very strongly on mathematicians being able to spot flaws.  It depends,
rather, on their ability to apply the rules of some formal system correctly
and constructively.

The difference is important.  Here's an analogy that may help explain why.

Consider the game of chess.  It's not at all surprising to meet people
who cannot play chess well; many people have deficiencies or "logical
illusions" that make them poor chess strategists.  It would be very
surprising, though, to meet anyone of normal intelligence who was incapable
(assuming some minimum exposure to the rules) of correctly declaring
whether a given sequence of moves were legal.

Now analogize the start position of chess to axioms, moves to legal proof
steps, and each legal position in chess to a theorem.  Even in *generating*
proofs (let alone checking another mathematician's proof) higher-level
"strategic" understanding is helpful, but it's not strictly necessary
(which is why automated theorem provers can be as good as they are --- some
have had performance as good as bright humans in nontrivial domains like
Euclidean plane geometry).

To establish that a proof is correct, it suffices to verify that each proof
tep is mechanically correct (in chess terms, that each move is legal).  Humans
do make mistakes at this, but the kind of mental blindness necessary to create
systematic blind spots would have so many other functional consequences that
we couldn't avoid noticing them --- semantic aphasia, inability to get the
same answer for `2 + 2' twice running, etc.

Or to put it another way:  applying the heuristics necessary to be `creative'
is a high-level cognitive task, easily screwed up by logical illusions.  
Proof checking, although often guided by the same kinds of heuristics, is
a low-level one and quite robust --- not much more difficult than (say)
double-entry bookkeeping.
-- 
					Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

