Date: Mon, 12 Apr 1993 01:34:02 -0400
From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: EVOL: Extropian Challenge (was New forms overtaking old  ...)

    I agree that all names but one will eventually die out, but it will
happen only under constraints strong enough to make the whole argument
not more than a statistical exercise.
 
    Memetics seems to be more interesting.
In many cases, the proliferation of a meme depends on its current share
in the corresponding niche.  In some cases, new/rare memes may find it
extremely difficult to spread among peers of about the same quality/use:
For example, a group of kids choosing a counting out rhime is more
likely to pick the one the majority of them know (unless the new one
is of much higher quality). Same with counter-clockwise clocks, etc.
>From the other hand, some memes thrive on rarity/novelty: consider
jokes, tunes, fashions...
     In the first case, we get a relatively stable meme population of
low diversity; in the second - very fluent and diverse.
 
     It seems possible to affect the memetic environment, and the
diversity/fluidity of the meme population by launching meta-memes
that would advocate either traditional or innovative approach in
a certain area.  Religions and modern industry have semi-consciously
evolved an ability to use this technique; most of other meme domains
haven't been [intentionally] touched at all.
Which means that a well-planned effort in memetic engineering might
cause great changes at very little expense...
 
 
