Date: Sun, 11 Apr 93 22:30:48 EDT
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: killer genes

   From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>

   How does hemoglobin kill its alleles??

(How does a business kill its competition?)

Usually, a gene kills its alleles by out-competing it.  For instance,
many mutations to hemoglobin produce a gene that is less useful as an
oxygen-carrying protein, and so those genes are killed by the
original.  The major exception is sickle-cell hemoglobin, which has
some selective advantages in areas with a high incidence of malaria.

Dale

