Date: Sun, 11 Apr 1993 23:36:22 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Extropian priorities

(P.S. Tim May come back!  Your posting exile is an imposition not
just on yourself, but on the many of us who like to read your
stuff!)

It amazes me to see so much bandwidth wasted on either-or questions
of priority.  Self-transformation and immortality are synergistic.
Let's see you try to make a backup without transforming yourself quite
substantially, or try to transform yourself after you are dead and
dispersed into the four winds.  Korzybski may have been cranky,
but this debate shows just how right he was, and I daresay that
very high in the self-transformation priority queue should be
a prolonged training in multi-valued logic and fuzzy sets, the
structural differential, etc., and incorporating these into our 
habits of thought and discourse.

That said, I weigh some aspects higher than others.  Specifically,
I forego trying to predict the long-term future of computational
power, "The Singularity", etc. except in the broadest way dealing
with the most important aspects (cf. my posts on uploading &
sexuality, for example).  I put high priority on what I can
predict and control, and that means the short term.  For life
extension this means practicing dietary restriction & taking
antioxidants (C, E, beta carotene, etc.), and exploring
to improve more elaborate avenues like cryonics, genetic 
therapy, neural transplant, etc.  

Political stuff has recently left me cold.  Despite my criticisms
of individualism, I myself am quite individualist by temperment and
see little reason now to participate in idealistic, collectivist
ventures like the Libertarian Party which give me no benefit in return 
(at the very best, a long-term and speculative benefit which mostly
goes to free riders).  I'm coming around to a more individualist way,
that is influencing the impact of politics at a more personal level,
making freedom for myself in an unfree world.  For example, digital 
capitalism (cf. recent Forbes article, Robin Hanson's idea futures, 
Richard Sandor's pollution and insurance futures, etc.), cypherpunks 
stuff, etc.  Of course these are still collectivist group ventures,
but the payback for myself is more short-term and immediate, and
setting up low transaction cost markets, crypto services, t*x havens,
etc. provides business opportunities instead of arenas for endless
political wrangling.  Along the same lines, I've suggested that 
Libertopia needs not a constitution, but a business plan.   

BTW, I'd like to volunteer my services to the most interesting 
efforts at on-line markets and crypto protection for same out
there.  C/C++, Unix, Windows/DOS, extensive hacking skills, send 
e-mail to me (or extropians or cypherpunks) and let me (us) know 
what you're working on.  Also, I'd be happy to write up a mini-FAQ 
on who is working on what, if folks e-mail me with a synopsis of their 
project(s). I can also work discretely.

At the same time I'm quite the technophile and love to
discuss and work on issues in nanotech, evolutionary programming,
space development, etc.  The EP has some good short-term promise
(business opportunities), the other stuff is mostly for fun, but
the lines are blurred here (eg short-term "nanotech" like
nanolitho, biotech, etc. can provide business opportunities, and
biotech suegues into life extension, etc.)  To improve my view
of the future, I like to mix tech work and speculation with
exploratory business planning, figuring out when various engineering
capabilities may become economically attractive via market
analysis, cash-flow spreadsheet, etc.

