From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: EVOL: Extropian Challenge (was New forms overtaking old  ...)

   From: Ed_Lane@novell.com (Ed Lane)

   Scenario 1:
   -----------

   In a small isolated town called "Dogpatch" a woman takes a man in
   marriage and assumes his last name.  All their [immediate] progeny also
   assume this same last name.

   Question:  Will everyone eventually have the same last name?

   Scenario 2:
   -----------

   In *the world* assume that the same marriage conventions of "Scenario
   1" exist.

   Question 1:  Will everyone eventually have the same last name?
   Bonus 1: If Q1 then *when* will it happen?
   Bonus 2: If Q1 then *what* will that name be?

If you assume that the village's population stays roughly constant,
and that last names are selectively neutral, names will be lost by
chance, and eventually everybody will have the same last name.  The
chance that a particular name is the winner is roughly proportional to
the number of people who have it at present.

However:  Names mutate, because people change their last names for
various reasons.  In Chinese culture, there is a prohibition against
"name incest", that is, the marriage of two people who bear the same
last name, so names aren't selectively neutral.

Making all sorts of unrealistic assumptions about the statistical
independence of various events, one can use standard formulas to
compute the statistics of when one-name-day will arrive.

   Followup Scenarios:
   -------------------

   How is the result of Scenarios 1 and 2 affected by:
	   1) polygamy (Utah?)
	   2) divorce/adoption (US-at-large?)
	   3) female fetalcide (India?)

Probably no difference, except that polygamy increases the variance of
the number of children than men have, speeding up name lossage.

   Extropian Scenario:
   -------------------

   What are the implications of "Scenario 2" to memetics?
   Do old memes [last names] ever die?

Well, most memes are not selectively neutral, at least, in the war
against allele memes.  But I'm sure that memes die; there are lots of
languages, cultures, and religions that are gone.

Dale

