From: cbmvax!snark.thyrsus.com!esr@uunet.UU.NET (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: American Intelligence

Recently I wrote:
> I'm a fan of the newer multi-factor psychometric systems.  But I'd love to
> take an IQ test designed for the six- to nine-sigma range sometime, just to
> see what it says.  Anybody know what range the Mensa tests cover, and how
> well?

Quite by accident, I found out tonight that my good friend Paula Matuszek is
an expert on statistical pychometrics (she still gets royalties from a book
she wrote on the subject).  She told me a couple of interesting things.

1) One sigma of IQ deviation is about 15 points.

2) Mensa's threshold is two sigmas above average, or IQ 130.

3) Standard tests max out at about three sigmas.  The reason for this is
interesting.  No, it's *not* because very high IQ involves unmeasurable
varieties of ability or is otherwise will-defined. 

It turns out that almost nobody's ever *needed* to discriminate systematically
between people with IQs of more than three sigmas off mean!  If your IQ is
145 or above, you have enough smarts to handle just about any problem the kinds
of institutions that fund IQ tests are normally organized to deal with.

She says the closest thing to an IQ test for populations above that range is
the GRE, because graduate schools actually need to pick and choose between
people in that ability range.  

4) The SAT-to-IQ correlation is plausible, though she'd need to see figures.
SATs and IQ tests are similar in that they're designed to repeatably measure
the kinds of abilities that help people do well in school, they're both fit
to a Gaussian distribution, and they're normed on similar populations.

Contrary to journalistic myth, it seems they're fairly good at predicting
this (that is, IQ/SAT really does measure something besides the ability to
score high on IQ/SAT tests).  This is sometimes obscured by the way they're
used.  For example (she said), SAT score is not a good predictor of
performance among students accepted at MIT --- but that's because MIT uses
it as a high-pass filter, simply not admitting anyone who isn't smart
enough for their performance there to depend on less measurable factors!

5) The test report I saw on me probably *was* from a Stanford-Binet --- it turns
out that that test battery yields a `verbal-intelligence' and a `performance'
score.

6) Mine were 145 and 127 respectively.  She said that kind of divergence is
normal, and expectable for me because the performance part of the test is
tightly timed and somewhat dependent on good coordination; thus the results
were probably, in some sense, distorted by my cerebral palsy.

7) As my measured IQ is right at the threshold where the tests become
unreliable, I'll probably never know very precisely where I sit on the bell
curve.  My own guess had been that my actual IQ was around 155, based on
comparisons with IQs I've seen attributed to various well-known people.

Accordingly, I told her that I'd guessed I was somewhere between 145 and 160
(what I now know is the three- to four-sigma range).  She laughed and said
that was probably way low, a reaction I found more than a bit surprising (and,
yes, gratifying).  She's not the type to flatter.

So I guessed the sigma size wrong, but I was probably right nevertheless in
wishing for a six- to nine-sigma instrument.  Unfortunately none such exists.

8) Her opinion of `affect' psychometrics like the Myers-Briggs system is that
they're generally too poorly instrumented to be statistically credible.  This
is not the same as saying they're junk, though; they can be valuable organizing
devices for clinicians.

More than this, though, she says human beings are very perceptive observers
of other human beings' abilities (this certainly makes evolutionary sense).
It doesn't take much of an organizing device to focus this; even a Tarot deck
can do it (yes, this is *her* example, not mine!).

All in all, a very interesting conversation.  Next time we get together I'm
going to ask her what she thinks about Jensen's Q.

(More about Paula?  She's an AI researcher with a postgraduate degree in
educational psychology, and I think another in CS.  In her late forties,
raising four gifted kids, married to another genius.  SF fan.  Very extropian;
she'd be at home here if she had the time.  Bakes challa to die for.)
-- 
					Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

