Date: Sun, 11 Apr 93 21:45:27 EDT
From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: Economic systems

I wrote the following essay.  I hope you find it interesting.

			   Economic Systems

This is a short essay on economic systems, that is, the structures
that societies use to organize and control the creation, distribution,
and consumption of the necessities of life.  It is put together from
information from many sources, and is of course over-simplified.  But
it should serve as an introduction to a rather fascinating aspect of
human culture that is rarely discussed.

There seem to be three major economic systems: tradition, command, and
market.  Of course, no society uses one of these forms exclusively,
and none of these is ever present in a pure form, but they a good set
of categories.

The oldest system is tradition -- society is organized as a set of
interlocking obligations between individuals.  The obligations are set
by tradition; nobody has the right or authority to change them,
although they do evolve slowly over time.  Usually, the obligations
between individuals are based on kinship relationships.

It seems that all societies that exist at "pre-state" levels of
complexity are based on tradition, although some elements of the other
systems are present.  (Beware of confusing reciprocal gift-giving
relationships with market transactions -- since the goal of a
gift-giving relationship is to maintain a social relationship via
alternating exchanges of gifts, there never is a quid-pro-quo exchange
that reaches a state of completion.)  Tradition-based systems usually
appear in societies that do not have social or occupational classes,
because there is no method of deliberately balancing the needs of the
various classes.  However, I've seen reports that in early Indian
villages, the various castes were hereditary occupational classes
which distributed the products of their labors and received the
products of the other castes.

The earliest civilizations (that is, societies with a state
organization) added the innovation of a command economy -- goods were
produced at the command of the state (or extracted in taxes by the
state), and redistributed by the command of the state.  This had
distinct advantages over tradition-based economies.  First, the
structure of the economy could be deliberately changed by decree of
the rulers.  Second, it was much easier to accommodate social and
occupational classes, because the command structure could be
deliberately adjusted to balance the needs of the various classes.
Third, the rulers could concentrate large resources on the
accomplishment of specific projects.

One of the specific projects that rulers expended large efforts on was
warfare, which explains the growth of early civilizations at the
expense of the unorganized peoples around them.  However, the overhead
of maintaining the administrative structure of a state usually causes
the state to succumb unless the state is capable of accomplishing
something beneficial that unorganized peoples cannot.  In the case of
the early civilizations, that usually was the construction of large
irrigation systems.  In areas with poor rainfall, a good irrigation
system gives a society a large advantage, and tradition-based
societies cannot maintain a large irrigation system.

The modern Communist governments have attempted to implement the
command system in industrial economies.  They seemed to be reasonably
successful at it until the rapid economic and technological changes of
the last twenty years exceeded the abilities of the administrators to
keep up.

The third great economic system is the market.  A good outline can be
quoted from McEvedy:

	`The Lydians were the first nation to introduce the use of
	gold and silver coins, and the first who sold goods by
	retail.'  So says Herodotus; and as for a century or more
	before the first issue of gold and silver coins (by Croesus,
	550 [B.C.]) the Lydians had had a currency of hallmarked
	electrum slugs the beginnings of the market economy can be put
	back to about 700 [B.C.].  Living in that artificial
	construct, a free society, and regarding the regulation of
	price by supply and demand as something akin to a natural law,
	it is difficult for us to visualize the economic processes of
	primitive communities.  Their essential feature is a sort of
	rationing system that is not egalitarian but hierarchical; you
	are allowed three strings of beads if and when you are
	entitled to wear them.  Consumption is both conspicuous and
	mandatory and its primary purpose is to express rank.  The
	Communists have partly reverted to this system in protest at
	the destructive social effects of laissez-faire economics, but
	the price of re-triblaization is liberty.  Conversely, the
	liberation of the individual from the social mould was the
	dynamic behind the seventh century upsurge of the Aegean
	peoples.  In contrast to the towns of Mesopotamia which had
	no market places (Herodotus again, confirmed by archaeology),
	the agora was the recognized centre of the Greek city.  With a
	fistful of coins and an eye for the main chance, the
	individual had arrived in history.
	[Colin McEvedy, "The Penguin Atlas of Ancient History"
	(Penguin, 1967), p. 54]

Actually, McEvedy's description is not strictly accurate, since
Mesopotamian law codes (c. 2000 B.C.) show that quid-pro-quo market
transactions existed before coined money, but the subjects of the laws
suggest that the society was based more on feudal-style relationships
than open market transactions, and prices were often set by law.
There are numerous laws discussing the renting of fields, orchards,
oxen, and boats, but the only types of merchants discussed are wine
sellers, moneylenders, and travelling traders and peddlers.

The great advantage of the market economy is the power of the market
as an information-processing system -- prices rapidly and effectively
communicate the relative need that society has for various goods, and
direct each producer and consumer into the most economically efficient
course of action by pursuit of material gain.  However, the
development of a market economy is much more difficult than we
realize.  It took Europe 500 years or more (1000 to 1500) to work out
the details.  A functioning market economy requires systems of customs
and laws to define property rights, courts to enforce them, systems to
block the formation of monopolies, a "social security" system to
support people who produce less than they need to live on, etc.  More
subtly, an individual in a market economy is (must be) driven by
pursuit of material gain, to a degree which is not present in
non-market economies.  (Consider that most traditional religions
(which solidified in non-market cultures) forbid "greed".)

In addition, a market system only works smoothly in the circumstances
that an economist would call an "efficient market".  The most
important of these are the presence of enough buyers and sellers that
transactions set well-defined market prices.  In many pre-modern
societies the transportation systems were so bad that shipping many
goods from even one village to the next was economically infeasible,
dramatically limiting the number market participants.

Interestingly, the conditions that prevent an efficient market are
usually the *absence* of some facility, that is, technological advance
usually facilitates efficient markets, rather than preventing them.
Thus one may look at the maxim that "Capitalism is inevitable." not as
describing capitalism as the original state of the human race (which
was traditional, not market) but rather as the consequence of
technological development.

An interesting question is to speculate on possible future economic
systems that may succeed the market.  There may well be one, and it is
probably already present in the modern world in some form or other,
but it is probably impossible to discover in advance of its ascent.

All societies seem to have a mix of the three different systems,
although one or the other is dominant.  Even in modern American
culture, there are large sectors that are controlled by traditional or
command systems.  The modern welfare system and public works are
supported by taxation, which is a command system.  The deep set of
tradtions that form the base of "industrial, democratic society" are a
traditional system, as is the system of relationships within the
family.

Another aspect of economic systems worth noting is the relative
freedom of the individual.  Clearly, the market system provides the
most range for action, since the individual can construct a unique
role for himself, limited only by needing an income larger than his
expenditures.  In command or traditional systems, the individual is
restricted to a role that is defined externally to himself.  However,
the command system is somewhat freer than the traditional system,
since in a command system, there is at least someone to negotiate with
-- the individual has the possibility of cutting a deal with the
rulers that he considers advantageous.  In a traditional system, there
are no exemptions to the set roles, because there is no one to grant
an exemption.

Conversely, the sectors of modern life that allow the most individual
freedom are the ones that produce the least vicious political fights,
since an individual can exercise personal choice.  The sectors of
modern life that have the least freedom, such as family relationships
and sex-role relationships, are the ones that produce the most vicious
political fights, because the role of the idividual is fixed by
society.  This would suggest that all of these relationships be
relegated to the market, but there are severe difficulties with
that -- a newborn baby is not a profitable actor in the market, and it
is difficult to render the long-term interdependencies of family in
terms of the market, which generally presumes that relationships are
subject to periodic re-bidding.

Dale

